    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "cd47938e_e1d562a3",
        "filename": "net/netlink_utils.h",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 128,
      "author": {
        "id": 1081399
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-12-01T00:49:24Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I believe these comments are erroneous, and probably better left out.\n\nErrors:\n- out_scan_capabilities is not a list at all\n- out_frature_flags is incorrect with respect to both: the parameter name, and the parameter type\n\nTo be fair, the first the comment isn\u0027t erroneous as such. But the first comment is confusing nonetheless. When I read that a pointer argument \"is a list\", I assume that the parameter must be pointing at an array. But that is not the case here.\n\nMore generally: it seems like all these comments do is to state the type information. But I don\u0027t know why we should do that. We\u0027ve already got the type information present in the function prototype.\n\nAnd importantly, the type information in prototype is verified by the compiler. So we know it is correct (unlike comments).\n\nI\u0027m guessing that these comments were motivated by the part of the style guide that lists inputs and outputs as things that might be mentioned in a function comment. [1] But that section also says we should state the obvious. And I argue that the comments above are just repeating type information, which _is_ obvious.\n\n[1] https://google.github.io/styleguide/cppguide.html#Function_Comments",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 124,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 128,
        "endChar": 45
      },
      "revId": "dfd1fb6a651ccb0d057c238c691c7e7dcb949464",
